WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:03.603 line:0%
-I'm inclined to have you argue
your motion,

00:00:03.603 --> 00:00:06.372 line:0%
and then I'll consider
late-arriving petitions.

00:00:06.372 --> 00:00:08.441 line:0%
-I'm sorry for wasting
the court's time, your honor,

00:00:08.441 --> 00:00:11.711 line:0%
but it's impossible for me to
continue with this hearing.

00:00:11.711 --> 00:00:13.980 line:0%
-And why is that, Ms. Wexler?

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:15.815
-Because I'm no longer
an attorney.

00:00:18.851 --> 00:00:21.187
-I'm sorry. What?

00:00:21.187 --> 00:00:23.757
-I gave notice to the bar
two hours ago.

00:00:28.127 --> 00:00:30.096
-Hey, welcome back, everybody.

00:00:30.096 --> 00:00:31.097
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:31.097 --> 00:00:32.165
We're here with Rhea Seehorn.

00:00:32.165 --> 00:00:34.200
That was a clip
from "Better Call Saul."

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:37.670
You directed a wonderful episode
this year.

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:39.906
So you're directing
your roommates --

00:00:39.906 --> 00:00:40.974
not just your co-stars,
but your roommates.

00:00:40.974 --> 00:00:42.942
-I did.
-How was that when you came home

00:00:42.942 --> 00:00:45.144
after a long day's work
with your cast?

00:00:45.144 --> 00:00:48.848
-Well, I mean we already talk
about the scenes,

00:00:48.848 --> 00:00:51.117
as I said, ad nauseam, anyway.

00:00:51.117 --> 00:00:55.020
So I kind of had it cleared
with them to discuss the scenes

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:57.023
we did.
That was episode 604.

00:00:57.023 --> 00:01:00.360
So Patrick had the great therapy
scene as Hamlin

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:04.063
and it was when Jimmy's dressed
as Hamlin,

00:01:04.063 --> 00:01:06.432
driving his car with Wendy
and all of that,

00:01:06.432 --> 00:01:08.668
and so we discussed them
at length,

00:01:08.668 --> 00:01:09.936
but by the time we got to set,
yeah,

00:01:09.936 --> 00:01:11.137
they kind of laughed in my face.

00:01:11.137 --> 00:01:12.839
-How did you --
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:12.839 --> 00:01:14.541
-And you also had to direct
yourself

00:01:14.541 --> 00:01:15.608
a great deal on that episode.
-Yeah.

00:01:15.608 --> 00:01:17.210
-That was a big Kim episode.

00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:20.847
Did you find that difficult or
was it nice to sort of take

00:01:20.847 --> 00:01:24.517
your mind out of both places
in order to do the whole job?

00:01:24.517 --> 00:01:28.287
-It is challenging, and I did
some press where people were

00:01:28.287 --> 00:01:30.457
like, "Wow, you really wanted to
direct yourself a lot,

00:01:30.457 --> 00:01:32.325
'cause there's huge Kim scenes
in that."

00:01:32.325 --> 00:01:33.559
But that's not what it was.

00:01:33.559 --> 00:01:35.628
It was just this jigsaw puzzle
of the calendar

00:01:35.628 --> 00:01:38.030
of which directors can leave
the writer's room.

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:39.932
We have a lot of
writer-directors

00:01:39.932 --> 00:01:44.103
and producer-directors, and so
I just got the episode I got.

00:01:44.103 --> 00:01:46.072
But they put -- you know,
they put a huge infrastructure

00:01:46.072 --> 00:01:48.908
in place, like there's no time
to watch playback,

00:01:48.908 --> 00:01:50.409
plus that would --
-Yeah.

00:01:50.409 --> 00:01:53.112
-I know there's some actors
that watch themselves

00:01:53.112 --> 00:01:54.647
and then go back to set.
I could never.

00:01:54.647 --> 00:01:56.048
I could never.
-Yeah.

00:01:56.048 --> 00:01:57.116
-It's just like you're just
constantly reminded of like,

00:01:57.116 --> 00:01:58.918
"Oh, that's your face."
-Oh, 100%.

00:01:58.918 --> 00:02:00.186
-That's what it still looks
like.

00:02:00.186 --> 00:02:01.754
-Oh, yeah.
I would walk out and be like,

00:02:01.754 --> 00:02:06.059
"My only note is I think we need
to stop doing this forever."

00:02:06.059 --> 00:02:07.927
-100%, yeah.

00:02:07.927 --> 00:02:10.497
Like, I can feel good about the
choices I'm making as an actor,

00:02:10.497 --> 00:02:13.766
but for anybody old enough to
remember answering machines,

00:02:13.766 --> 00:02:16.202
that very first time you made
the voicemail and you hear

00:02:16.202 --> 00:02:18.738
your voice
and you're so alarmed.

00:02:18.738 --> 00:02:21.641
It's like, "Seriously? That is
what everyone's listening to?"

00:02:21.641 --> 00:02:24.477
-Yeah, you're like, "It sounded
like this the whole time?"

00:02:24.477 --> 00:02:26.980
-Yeah, no one told me?
-And nobody told me?

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:27.980
[ Laughter ]

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:29.148
-Nobody has said,
"Seth, seriously,

00:02:29.148 --> 00:02:30.817
don't ever speak again."
That's what it's like.

00:02:30.817 --> 00:02:32.185
-It's only because you would
leave a voicemail

00:02:32.185 --> 00:02:34.287
and you assumed that your
friends would call and be like,

00:02:34.287 --> 00:02:35.587
"Hey, has something happened
to you?

00:02:35.587 --> 00:02:37.256
You sound super [bleep] up
on that voicemail."

00:02:37.256 --> 00:02:38.390
[ Laughter ]

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:39.559
But they're all just like,
"Hey, it's me.

00:02:39.559 --> 00:02:41.260
Just calling."
Like nobody's taken aback, yeah.

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:43.629
-Because they're like, "Seth has
sounded like crap forever."

00:02:43.629 --> 00:02:44.664
-Forever.
-That's always the way

00:02:44.664 --> 00:02:45.832
he sounded like.

00:02:45.832 --> 00:02:49.101
-Are you relieved that
you're almost now where,

00:02:49.101 --> 00:02:52.105
you know, I guess four episodes
away from you not having

00:02:52.105 --> 00:02:54.107
to worry about giving anything
away?

00:02:54.107 --> 00:02:56.976
Because the longer people have
watched this show,

00:02:56.976 --> 00:02:59.445
the more I feel they've been
desperate

00:02:59.445 --> 00:03:01.247
to see how everything ends.
-Yeah.

00:03:01.247 --> 00:03:02.615
-And you know.

00:03:02.615 --> 00:03:05.017
And has it been exhausting
trying to keep those secrets

00:03:05.017 --> 00:03:07.153
or has it been fun?

00:03:07.153 --> 00:03:10.189
-It's been fun, because even
when people do say,

00:03:10.189 --> 00:03:12.959
"So what happens to Kim?"
Or whatever,

00:03:12.959 --> 00:03:14.193
they don't actually want you
to answer.

00:03:14.193 --> 00:03:16.162
They would stop me
even if I started to speak.

00:03:16.162 --> 00:03:17.630
-Yeah.

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:21.000
-But I am looking forward to
having a much bigger book club.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.769
It's like I read this book by
myself and I don't get to talk

00:03:23.769 --> 00:03:25.838
to anybody about what'd
you think of the ending?

00:03:25.838 --> 00:03:31.777
So as we roll out these episodes
and the remaining episodes,

00:03:31.777 --> 00:03:34.013
of course, I can't spoil whether
you'll see me again or not,

00:03:34.013 --> 00:03:35.481
but they're bananas.

00:03:35.481 --> 00:03:40.720
Nobody actually tracked exactly
how thoughtfully and intricately

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:43.189
this comes together and the
different storylines

00:03:43.189 --> 00:03:45.524
that play out,
so I'm looking forward

00:03:45.524 --> 00:03:47.125
to talking to people about it.

00:03:47.125 --> 00:03:49.361
-It was such a cool show
because, you know, when I heard

00:03:49.361 --> 00:03:51.497
they were doing a prequel to
"Breaking Bad", you know,

00:03:51.497 --> 00:03:52.831
I was like,
"That seems strange,"

00:03:52.831 --> 00:03:56.101
and it has been -- I didn't --
I will just say I thought

00:03:56.101 --> 00:03:57.803
it was a bad idea.
-Yeah, sure.

00:03:57.803 --> 00:04:01.307
-And I can say that now because
my instincts are often wrong

00:04:01.307 --> 00:04:02.675
and I was totally wrong here.

00:04:02.675 --> 00:04:04.510
And it was so great to again see
cast members

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:06.913
from "Breaking Bad"
that we love.

00:04:06.913 --> 00:04:09.415
I mean, what you did, what
Patrick did, what Michael Mando

00:04:09.415 --> 00:04:11.250
did -- like it was so wonderful
to see.

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:13.952
I'm really deeply sad it's
almost over, but I can't wait to

00:04:13.952 --> 00:04:15.788
see it, and I'm so happy
for you.

00:04:15.788 --> 00:04:17.423
-Thanks.
-Thank you for being back again.

00:04:17.423 --> 00:04:20.560
You guys, one more time,
Rhea Seehorn.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:23.796
A new episode of the last season
of "Better Call Saul" airs

00:04:23.796 --> 00:04:25.397
this Monday at 9:00 P.M. on AMC.

00:04:25.397 --> 00:04:26.899
We'll be right back
with Jeremy Allen White.

